>  Consider that serial ascenders don't often play the protection racket.

Question: How do serial ascenders manage the AC/weight/spellcasting balance?
I'll be the first one to say that the protection racket doesn't ensure
ascensions. But I feel that it does give me more flexibility to balance that
set of attributes in the mid game. Later on it doesn't matter as both magical 
and divine AC is available later. I mean who needs armor when you can wear a
+5 tee-shirt? But in the early game options are somewhat limited. You don't
necessarily know enchant armor in the early game. You don't have holy water
in the early game. You don't necessarily have access to a magic marker in
the early game. Or have access to an altar and an unlimited supply of monsters.
All that is available later. But it seems like in the early game that AC
is available from armor and bought protection. Since serial ascenders do not
target buying protection early (I know they can get it later) what do they
do to ensure having sufficient AC to manage, and more importantly how do they
obtain that AC in such a way that it doesn't completely screw up their weight
or spellcasting.

Maybe my view is tinged by the fact that I favor spellcasters. I've ascended
a wizard, healer, and archeologist. I'm playing a monk now. I don't usually do
the combat wombat thing. So spellcasting is an important part of my arsenal.
Whenever I see a piece of armor my two automatic questions are "How much
does it weigh?" and "How does it affect my spellcasting failure rate?"

Are serial ascenders generally combat wombats? I always read that Valkeries are
the easiest to ascend. Excellent strength and con, but can't cast spells worth
shit. They have little need for a protection run.

But armor screws with game balance badly for spellcasters. They are generally
weaker characters with less strength and less constitution. So they have
less hit points and can carry less. Spells are their great equalizers, but
armor generally screws it up. That's why dragon scale mail (yet another
out of reach item in the early game) is so coveted. Light, great AC, no
spellcasting effect.

So I think I stick with the protection racket because it positively impacts AC
without the negative effects for spellcasting.

>  Avoiding leveling up is a riskier strategy than not avoiding leveling up.

Agreed. It's a bad move in the long term. But like everything else in this
game it has to be managed. You have to level up smart. You have to know that
when you level up that you'll get neagtive impacts and you have to be 
prepared for those impacts. Get your AC together. Make sure you have the
right resistances. Get your pet management down.

>  Then again
>pursuing leveling up to the point you're competing with your pet
>is also riskier as well.

I let the pet go first for a couple of reasons. First is that they have a
hard ceiling of XL9 and 72HP. Second is that they are even more vulnerable 
than you are because they can't improve their AC. Third is that the RNG
bases monster XL generated on you, not your pet, so you can create a huge
advantage. 4th is that you level up to reasonable levels fast. During the 
run I have to work real hard not to get to XL4 or XL5. You can do that just
bumping into monsters.

